(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("k8s-control-plane",{"k8s-control-plane/the-request-path-authentication-authorization-and-admission":{"html":"\u003cp>Every change to a Kubernetes cluster is an HTTPS request to the API server. The server does not simply write what it receives. It passes each request through a fixed series of stages, and knowing the order explains most permission errors, webhook outages and surprising defaults.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The order of stages: authentication, authorization, mutating admission, schema validation, validating admission, storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How each stage can say no, and what the error looks like\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why admission webhooks are powerful and risky\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to read the stage from an error message\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authentication-who-is-asking\">Authentication: who is asking\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The API server first works out the identity. It tries each configured authenticator until one succeeds: client certificates, bearer tokens (service account tokens), OpenID Connect tokens, or a webhook. If none succeeds, the request is rejected with 401 Unauthorized. A successful authenticator produces a user name, groups and extra attributes. Nothing about permissions is decided yet. A request with no credentials can still become the user \u003ccode>system:anonymous\u003c/code> if anonymous access is enabled, which is why hardening guides turn it off.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"authorization-may-they-do-this\">Authorization: may they do this\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Next the server asks the authorizers whether this identity may perform this verb on this resource in this namespace. A kubeadm cluster uses Node authorization and RBAC. The first authorizer that allows the request wins, and if none allows it the answer is 403 Forbidden. The message names the user, the verb, the resource and the namespace, which is usually enough to write the missing rule. You can test any identity with \u003ccode>kubectl auth can-i\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"admission-may-the-object-be-changed-or-accepted\">Admission: may the object be changed or accepted\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Only an authorized request reaches admission. Mutating admission plugins and webhooks run first and may change the object, for example by adding a sidecar or a default value. The server then validates the object against its schema. Validating admission runs last and can only accept or reject. Built-in plugins include LimitRanger, ResourceQuota, ServiceAccount and Pod Security. Admission webhooks let your own service make the decision, and ValidatingAdmissionPolicy lets you write rules in CEL without running a service.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-and-the-response\">Storage and the response\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If every stage passes, the API server writes the object to etcd and returns the stored version with its new resourceVersion. Controllers then see the change through watches. Reads skip the admission stages, but still go through authentication and authorization.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-errors-by-stage\">Reading errors by stage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A 401 is authentication, a 403 is authorization, and a message that starts with &quot;admission webhook ... denied the request&quot; or &quot;admission plugin ... denied&quot; is admission. A 422 usually means the object failed schema validation. A timeout on every create is a classic sign of a webhook that is slow or unreachable and configured with failurePolicy Fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as jane\nkubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations\nkubectl get mutatingwebhookconfigurations\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as jane\nkubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations\nkubectl get mutatingwebhookconfigurations\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"client&gt;authn;authn&gt;authz;authz&gt;mut;mut&gt;val;val&gt;etcd\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The request path: authentication, authorization and admission\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"8.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"25.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"25.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"41.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"41.7\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"58.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"58.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"75.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"75.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"91.7\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"client\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:8.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Client\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"authn\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:25.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Authentication\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"authz\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:41.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Authorization\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mut\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:58.3%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Mutating admission\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"val\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:75.0%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Validating admission\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"etcd\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:91.7%;top:75.0%;--w:14.7%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">etcd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Client to Authentication; 2, Authentication to Authorization; 3, Authorization to Mutating admission; 4, Mutating admission to Validating admission; 5, Validating admission to etcd.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"client\">\u003cdt>Client \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends the request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong credentials.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"authn\">\u003cdt>Authentication \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Identifies the user.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Anonymous allowed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"authz\">\u003cdt>Authorization \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> RBAC and Node rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too broad roles.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mut\">\u003cdt>Mutating admission \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> May change the object.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Webhook slow or down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"val\">\u003cdt>Validating admission \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Accepts or rejects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Policy blocks valid change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"etcd\">\u003cdt>etcd \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores the object.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No quorum.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-webhook-that-blocked-every-pod\">Incident: The webhook that blocked every pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security webhook went down and nobody could create pods anywhere in the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl run test --image=nginx\nkubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations policy -o jsonpath='{.webhooks[0].failurePolicy}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl run \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> --image=nginx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server (InternalError): Internal error occurred: failed calling webhook &quot;policy.example.com&quot;: Post &quot;https://policy.svc:443/validate&quot;: dial tcp 10.0.4.12:443: connect: connection refused\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations policy -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.webhooks[0].failurePolicy}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Fail\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The webhook had failurePolicy Fail, so while its pods were down the API server refused every matching request, including the pods needed to bring the webhook back. The recovery was to delete or edit the webhook configuration so it ignored failures. Scope webhooks with namespace selectors, exclude the namespace that runs the webhook itself, and prefer failurePolicy Ignore for non-critical checks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading a 403 as an admission problem, or the reverse\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving a webhook failurePolicy Fail without excluding its own namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming reads go through admission\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that mutating changes happen before validation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting broad RBAC to fix a webhook error\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"authentication-who-is-asking","text":"Authentication: who is asking"},{"id":"authorization-may-they-do-this","text":"Authorization: may they do this"},{"id":"admission-may-the-object-be-changed-or-accepted","text":"Admission: may the object be changed or accepted"},{"id":"storage-and-the-response","text":"Storage and the response"},{"id":"reading-errors-by-stage","text":"Reading errors by stage"},{"id":"incident-the-webhook-that-blocked-every-pod","text":"Incident: The webhook that blocked every pod"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Controlling access to the Kubernetes API","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/controlling-access/"},{"t":"Admission controllers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/admission-controllers/"}],"text":"Every change to a Kubernetes cluster is an HTTPS request to the API server. The server does not simply write what it receives. It passes each request through a fixed series of stages, and knowing the order explains most permission errors, webhook outages and surprising defaults. What you will learn The order of stages: authentication, authorization, mutating admission, schema validation, validating admission, storage How each stage can say no, and what the error looks like Why admission webhooks are powerful and risky How to read the stage from an error message Authentication: who is asking The API server first works out the identity. It tries each configured authenticator until one succeeds: client certificates, bearer tokens (service account tokens), OpenID Connect tokens, or a webhook. If none succeeds, the request is rejected with 401 Unauthorized. A successful authenticator produces a user name, groups and extra attributes. Nothing about permissions is decided yet. A request with no credentials can still become the user system:anonymous if anonymous access is enabled, which is why hardening guides turn it off. Authorization: may they do this Next the server asks the authorizers whether this identity may perform this verb on this resource in this namespace. A kubeadm cluster uses Node authorization and RBAC. The first authorizer that allows the request wins, and if none allows it the answer is 403 Forbidden. The message names the user, the verb, the resource and the namespace, which is usually enough to write the missing rule. You can test any identity with kubectl auth can-i . Admission: may the object be changed or accepted Only an authorized request reaches admission. Mutating admission plugins and webhooks run first and may change the object, for example by adding a sidecar or a default value. The server then validates the object against its schema. Validating admission runs last and can only accept or reject. Built-in plugins include LimitRanger, ResourceQuota, ServiceAccount and Pod Security. Admission webhooks let your own service make the decision, and ValidatingAdmissionPolicy lets you write rules in CEL without running a service. Storage and the response If every stage passes, the API server writes the object to etcd and returns the stored version with its new resourceVersion. Controllers then see the change through watches. Reads skip the admission stages, but still go through authentication and authorization. Reading errors by stage A 401 is authentication, a 403 is authorization, and a message that starts with \"admission webhook ... denied the request\" or \"admission plugin ... denied\" is admission. A 422 usually means the object failed schema validation. A timeout on every create is a classic sign of a webhook that is slow or unreachable and configured with failurePolicy Fail. kubectl auth can-i create pods -n dev --as jane kubectl get validatingwebhookconfigurations kubectl get mutatingwebhookconfigurations The request path: authentication, authorization and admission Flow in order: 1, Client to Authentication; 2, Authentication to Authorization; 3, Authorization to Mutating admission; 4, Mutating admission to Validating admission; 5, Validating admission to etcd. Outside party Security Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Client Outside party What it does: Sends the request. If it fails or is weak: Wrong credentials. Authentication Security What it does: Identifies the user. If it fails or is weak: Anonymous allowed. Authorization Security What it does: RBAC and Node rules. If it fails or is weak: Too broad roles. Mutating admission Step What it does: May change the object. If it fails or is weak: Webhook slow or down. Validating admission Step What it does: Accepts or rejects. If it fails or is weak: Policy blocks valid change. etcd Data store What it does: Stores the object. If it fails or is weak: No quorum. Incid"},"k8s-control-plane/api-machinery-versions-watches-and-server-side-apply":{"html":"\u003cp>The API server is a typed, versioned store with a change feed. Controllers, kubectl and operators all rely on the same few ideas: resources have versions, every object has a resourceVersion, and clients can watch for changes instead of polling. This lesson explains those ideas and why they matter for cluster stability.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>API groups, versions and how conversion keeps old clients working\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>resourceVersion, list and watch, and why watches scale\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Optimistic concurrency and 409 conflicts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Server-side apply and field ownership\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>API Priority and Fairness protecting the server\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"groups-versions-and-deprecation\">Groups, versions and deprecation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Resources live in API groups such as \u003ccode>apps/v1\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>networking.k8s.io/v1\u003c/code>. A resource can be served in several versions at once, and the server converts between them. Stored objects use one storage version. Kubernetes deprecates versions on a published schedule, and \u003ccode>kubectl api-resources\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>kubectl api-versions\u003c/code> show what your cluster serves. Before an upgrade, check manifests and controllers for removed versions, because a removed version fails at apply time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"resourceversion-list-and-watch\">resourceVersion, list and watch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each object carries a resourceVersion that comes from etcd revisions. A client lists a resource type once, remembers the resourceVersion of the list, then opens a watch from that point. The server streams add, modify and delete events, so thousands of controllers can follow changes without polling. If a client falls too far behind, the watch returns an error saying the version is too old, and the client must list again. Informers in client libraries do this for you and keep a local cache.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"optimistic-concurrency\">Optimistic concurrency\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Updates include the resourceVersion the client last saw. If someone changed the object since, the server answers 409 Conflict and the client must read and retry. This is why two tools editing the same object sometimes collide. It is also why controllers re-read before writing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"server-side-apply\">Server-side apply\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With server-side apply the server tracks which manager owns each field. \u003ccode>kubectl apply --server-side\u003c/code> sends your intended fields, and the server merges them and reports a conflict if another manager owns a field you are changing. This makes it safe for several controllers to manage different parts of one object, and it is the preferred way for new tooling.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl api-resources --api-group=apps\nkubectl get deploy web -o yaml --show-managed-fields | head -40\nkubectl apply --server-side -f deploy.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl api-resources --api-group=apps\nkubectl get deploy web -o yaml --show-managed-fields | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -40\nkubectl apply --server-side -f deploy.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"api-priority-and-fairness\">API Priority and Fairness\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The server limits concurrent requests by sorting them into flows with priority levels, so one noisy client cannot starve system controllers. Clients that receive 429 Too Many Requests should back off. Metrics such as apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total show when limits are hit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ctl&gt;api;api&gt;apf;apf&gt;cache;cache&gt;etcd\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">API machinery: versions, watches and server-side apply\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"apf\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Priority and fairness\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cache\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Watch cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"etcd\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">etcd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Controller to API server; 2, API server to Priority and fairness; 3, Priority and fairness to Watch cache; 4, Watch cache to etcd.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctl\">\u003cdt>Controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lists then watches.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Polling in a loop.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Serves and converts versions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overloaded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"apf\">\u003cdt>Priority and fairness \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Queues by flow.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Misconfigured levels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cache\">\u003cdt>Watch cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Streams changes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Client too far behind.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"etcd\">\u003cdt>etcd \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Source of revisions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow disk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-controller-that-polled-instead-of-watched\">Incident: A controller that polled instead of watched\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A custom tool listed every pod once a second and slowed the whole API.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total | head -2\nkubectl get pods --all-namespaces --no-headers | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total{flow_schema=&quot;catch-all&quot;,priority_level=&quot;catch-all&quot;,reason=&quot;queue-full&quot;} 412\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods --all-namespaces --no-headers | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2840\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tool used a full list every second from a service account that fell into the catch-all flow, so its requests were queued and then rejected, and the extra load made other clients slower. Rewriting it with an informer, which lists once and then watches, cut the request rate by more than ninety percent. Always watch rather than poll, and give important controllers their own flow schema.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Polling the API with repeated list calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring 409 conflicts instead of re-reading\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Applying manifests that use removed API versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing client-side and server-side apply on the same fields\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not backing off after a 429\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"groups-versions-and-deprecation","text":"Groups, versions and deprecation"},{"id":"resourceversion-list-and-watch","text":"resourceVersion, list and watch"},{"id":"optimistic-concurrency","text":"Optimistic concurrency"},{"id":"server-side-apply","text":"Server-side apply"},{"id":"api-priority-and-fairness","text":"API Priority and Fairness"},{"id":"incident-a-controller-that-polled-instead-of-watched","text":"Incident: A controller that polled instead of watched"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"API concepts","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/api-concepts/"},{"t":"Server-side apply","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/server-side-apply/"}],"text":"The API server is a typed, versioned store with a change feed. Controllers, kubectl and operators all rely on the same few ideas: resources have versions, every object has a resourceVersion, and clients can watch for changes instead of polling. This lesson explains those ideas and why they matter for cluster stability. What you will learn API groups, versions and how conversion keeps old clients working resourceVersion, list and watch, and why watches scale Optimistic concurrency and 409 conflicts Server-side apply and field ownership API Priority and Fairness protecting the server Groups, versions and deprecation Resources live in API groups such as apps/v1 or networking.k8s.io/v1 . A resource can be served in several versions at once, and the server converts between them. Stored objects use one storage version. Kubernetes deprecates versions on a published schedule, and kubectl api-resources and kubectl api-versions show what your cluster serves. Before an upgrade, check manifests and controllers for removed versions, because a removed version fails at apply time. resourceVersion, list and watch Each object carries a resourceVersion that comes from etcd revisions. A client lists a resource type once, remembers the resourceVersion of the list, then opens a watch from that point. The server streams add, modify and delete events, so thousands of controllers can follow changes without polling. If a client falls too far behind, the watch returns an error saying the version is too old, and the client must list again. Informers in client libraries do this for you and keep a local cache. Optimistic concurrency Updates include the resourceVersion the client last saw. If someone changed the object since, the server answers 409 Conflict and the client must read and retry. This is why two tools editing the same object sometimes collide. It is also why controllers re-read before writing. Server-side apply With server-side apply the server tracks which manager owns each field. kubectl apply --server-side sends your intended fields, and the server merges them and reports a conflict if another manager owns a field you are changing. This makes it safe for several controllers to manage different parts of one object, and it is the preferred way for new tooling. kubectl api-resources --api-group=apps kubectl get deploy web -o yaml --show-managed-fields | head -40 kubectl apply --server-side -f deploy.yaml API Priority and Fairness The server limits concurrent requests by sorting them into flows with priority levels, so one noisy client cannot starve system controllers. Clients that receive 429 Too Many Requests should back off. Metrics such as apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total show when limits are hit. API machinery: versions, watches and server-side apply Flow in order: 1, Controller to API server; 2, API server to Priority and fairness; 3, Priority and fairness to Watch cache; 4, Watch cache to etcd. Service Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Controller Service What it does: Lists then watches. If it fails or is weak: Polling in a loop. API server Service What it does: Serves and converts versions. If it fails or is weak: Overloaded. Priority and fairness Step What it does: Queues by flow. If it fails or is weak: Misconfigured levels. Watch cache Data store What it does: Streams changes. If it fails or is weak: Client too far behind. etcd Data store What it does: Source of revisions. If it fails or is weak: Slow disk. Incident: A controller that polled instead of watched Incident A custom tool listed every pod once a second and slowed the whole API. $ kubectl get --raw /metrics | grep apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total | head -2 apiserver_flowcontrol_rejected_requests_total{flow_schema=\"catch-all\",priority_level=\"catch-all\",reason=\"queue-full\"} 412 $ kubectl get pods --all-namespaces --no-headers | wc -l 2840 (this is a simula"},"k8s-control-plane/etcd-data-model-raft-and-quorum":{"html":"\u003cp>etcd is the only stateful part of the control plane. If it is healthy the API server can be replaced freely. If it is lost without a backup, the cluster state is gone. Understanding how it stores data and agrees on writes makes sizing and recovery decisions straightforward.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How objects are stored as keys under /registry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Revisions and MVCC\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leader election and commit in Raft\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quorum maths and why odd member counts are used\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What disk and network latency do to the cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keys-and-revisions\">Keys and revisions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>etcd is a key-value store. The API server writes each object under a key such as \u003ccode>/registry/pods/default/web-1\u003c/code>, as a protobuf encoded value. Every write creates a new revision, a cluster-wide counter, and etcd keeps old revisions until they are compacted. This multi-version design is what makes watches possible, because a client can ask for all changes since a given revision. The resourceVersion you see in objects comes from these revisions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"raft-in-plain-words\">Raft in plain words\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Members of an etcd cluster elect one leader using the Raft algorithm. All writes go to the leader, which appends them to its write-ahead log and replicates them to followers. A write is committed when a majority of members have stored it. If the leader fails, the followers hold a new election after a timeout. Reads can be served linearizably through the leader, which is what the API server asks for by default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quorum\">Quorum\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A majority is floor(n/2) + 1. Three members tolerate one failure, five tolerate two, and seven tolerate three. An even number adds cost without adding tolerance: four members still tolerate one failure. When quorum is lost the cluster cannot commit writes, so the API server cannot change anything, although running pods keep running.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"latency-matters\">Latency matters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every commit waits for a disk sync on a majority of members, so slow disks directly slow the whole cluster. Use fast local SSD storage for etcd, keep members close in network terms, and avoid sharing the disk with noisy workloads. Slow fsync shows up as wal_fsync_duration warnings in the logs and as leader changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def tolerated(members):\n    quorum = members // 2 + 1\n    return members - quorum\n\nfor n in (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7):\n    print(n, &quot;members tolerate&quot;, tolerated(n), &quot;failure(s)&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">tolerated\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">members\u003c/span>):\n    quorum = members // \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> members - quorum\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(n, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;members tolerate&quot;\u003c/span>, tolerated(n), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failure(s)&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"look-at-it\">Look at it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status -w table --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status -w table --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The table shows the leader, the database size and the raft term for each member.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"api&gt;lead;lead&gt;f1;lead&gt;f2;lead&gt;wal\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">etcd: data model, Raft and quorum\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lead\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Leader\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"f1\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Follower\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"f2\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Follower\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wal\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">WAL on disk\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, API server to Leader; 2, Leader to Follower; 3, Leader to Follower; 4, Leader to WAL on disk.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes objects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cannot reach etcd.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lead\">\u003cdt>Leader \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Orders and replicates writes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slow disk causes elections.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"f1\">\u003cdt>Follower \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores the log.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lagging member.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"f2\">\u003cdt>Follower \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Stores the log.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost member.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wal\">\u003cdt>WAL on disk \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Synced before commit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> High fsync latency.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-of-three-members-down\">Incident: Two of three members down\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two etcd members failed in a single maintenance window and the API became read only in practice.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://10.0.0.1:2379,https://10.0.0.2:2379,https://10.0.0.3:2379\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://10.0.0.1:2379,https://10.0.0.2:2379,https://10.0.0.3:2379\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">https://10.0.0.1:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 8.2ms\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">https://10.0.0.2:2379 is unhealthy: failed to commit proposal: context deadline exceeded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">https://10.0.0.3:2379 is unhealthy: failed to commit proposal: context deadline exceeded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With one of three members left there was no quorum, so no writes could be committed and kubectl changes hung. Running pods were unaffected. The recovery was to bring at least one member back with its data, restoring quorum, then replace the other. If both members had lost their data, a snapshot restore would have been needed. Do maintenance one member at a time and check health between steps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running an even number of members\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting etcd on slow or shared disks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taking down two members at once for maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stretching members across high-latency links\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating etcd as a database for application data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"keys-and-revisions","text":"Keys and revisions"},{"id":"raft-in-plain-words","text":"Raft in plain words"},{"id":"quorum","text":"Quorum"},{"id":"latency-matters","text":"Latency matters"},{"id":"look-at-it","text":"Look at it"},{"id":"incident-two-of-three-members-down","text":"Incident: Two of three members down"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Operating etcd clusters for Kubernetes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"},{"t":"etcd documentation","u":"https://etcd.io/docs/"}],"text":"etcd is the only stateful part of the control plane. If it is healthy the API server can be replaced freely. If it is lost without a backup, the cluster state is gone. Understanding how it stores data and agrees on writes makes sizing and recovery decisions straightforward. What you will learn How objects are stored as keys under /registry Revisions and MVCC Leader election and commit in Raft Quorum maths and why odd member counts are used What disk and network latency do to the cluster Keys and revisions etcd is a key-value store. The API server writes each object under a key such as /registry/pods/default/web-1 , as a protobuf encoded value. Every write creates a new revision, a cluster-wide counter, and etcd keeps old revisions until they are compacted. This multi-version design is what makes watches possible, because a client can ask for all changes since a given revision. The resourceVersion you see in objects comes from these revisions. Raft in plain words Members of an etcd cluster elect one leader using the Raft algorithm. All writes go to the leader, which appends them to its write-ahead log and replicates them to followers. A write is committed when a majority of members have stored it. If the leader fails, the followers hold a new election after a timeout. Reads can be served linearizably through the leader, which is what the API server asks for by default. Quorum A majority is floor(n/2) + 1. Three members tolerate one failure, five tolerate two, and seven tolerate three. An even number adds cost without adding tolerance: four members still tolerate one failure. When quorum is lost the cluster cannot commit writes, so the API server cannot change anything, although running pods keep running. Latency matters Every commit waits for a disk sync on a majority of members, so slow disks directly slow the whole cluster. Use fast local SSD storage for etcd, keep members close in network terms, and avoid sharing the disk with noisy workloads. Slow fsync shows up as wal_fsync_duration warnings in the logs and as leader changes. def tolerated ( members ): quorum = members // 2 + 1 return members - quorum for n in ( 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 7 ): print (n, \"members tolerate\" , tolerated(n), \"failure(s)\" ) Look at it ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl endpoint status -w table --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key The table shows the leader, the database size and the raft term for each member. etcd: data model, Raft and quorum Flow in order: 1, API server to Leader; 2, Leader to Follower; 3, Leader to Follower; 4, Leader to WAL on disk. Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. API server Service What it does: Writes objects. If it fails or is weak: Cannot reach etcd. Leader Data store What it does: Orders and replicates writes. If it fails or is weak: Slow disk causes elections. Follower Data store What it does: Stores the log. If it fails or is weak: Lagging member. Follower Data store What it does: Stores the log. If it fails or is weak: Lost member. WAL on disk Data store What it does: Synced before commit. If it fails or is weak: High fsync latency. Incident: Two of three members down Incident Two etcd members failed in a single maintenance window and the API became read only in practice. $ etcdctl endpoint health --endpoints=https://10.0.0.1:2379,https://10.0.0.2:2379,https://10.0.0.3:2379 https://10.0.0.1:2379 is healthy: successfully committed proposal: took = 8.2ms https://10.0.0.2:2379 is unhealthy: failed to commit proposal: context deadline exceeded https://10.0.0.3:2379 is unhealthy: failed to commit proposal: context deadline exceeded (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) With one of three members left there was no quorum, so no writes could be committed and kubectl changes hung. Running pods were unaffect"},"k8s-control-plane/etcd-operations-health-compaction-and-defragmentation":{"html":"\u003cp>Most etcd incidents are boring and preventable: a database that grew past its quota, a slow disk, or a member that fell behind. This lesson covers the routine checks and the exact steps to recover from the most common failure, the space quota alarm.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading endpoint status, health and alarms\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why the database grows and what compaction does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defragmentation and when it is safe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The space quota alarm and how to clear it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Metrics worth alerting on\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"routine-checks\">Routine checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run three commands regularly, with the same endpoint and certificate flags you use for backups. \u003ccode>endpoint health\u003c/code> tests that each member can commit. \u003ccode>endpoint status\u003c/code> shows database size, leader and raft index for each member. \u003ccode>alarm list\u003c/code> shows any active alarm.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"export ETCDCTL_API=3\nE=&quot;--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key&quot;\netcdctl $E endpoint health\netcdctl $E endpoint status -w table\netcdctl $E alarm list\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> ETCDCTL_API=3\nE=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key&quot;\u003c/span>\netcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> endpoint health\netcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> endpoint status -w table\netcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> alarm list\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-the-database-grows\">Why the database grows\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Because etcd keeps old revisions, the data file grows with every change, and objects that change often, such as leases and events, create many revisions. Compaction discards revisions older than a chosen one. The API server compacts automatically on a schedule, by default every five minutes, so growth normally stays bounded.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compaction-is-not-defragmentation\">Compaction is not defragmentation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compaction marks old revisions as free, but the file on disk does not shrink. Free space inside the file is reused for new data. Defragmentation rewrites the file to give space back to the filesystem. It blocks the member while it runs, so defragment one member at a time, and not the leader first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"etcdctl $E defrag --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">etcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> defrag --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-space-quota-alarm\">The space quota alarm\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>etcd has a backend quota, 2 GiB by default and commonly raised to 8 GiB. If the data file reaches it, etcd raises a NOSPACE alarm and rejects writes, with the error &quot;mvcc: database space exceeded&quot;. Recovery is: find the current revision, compact to it, defragment each member, then disarm the alarm.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"REV=$(etcdctl $E endpoint status -w json | grep -o '&quot;revision&quot;:[0-9]*' | head -1 | cut -d: -f2)\netcdctl $E compact $REV\netcdctl $E defrag\netcdctl $E alarm disarm\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">REV=$(etcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> endpoint status -w json | grep -o \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&quot;revision&quot;:[0-9]*&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cut\u003c/span> -d: -f2)\netcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> compact \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$REV\u003c/span>\netcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> defrag\netcdctl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$E\u003c/span> alarm disarm\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-alert-on\">What to alert on\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Alert on database size close to the quota, on a rising rate of leader changes, on high wal_fsync and backend_commit latency, and on failed proposals. Also watch the number of objects, since a runaway controller that creates many events or secrets is the usual cause of growth.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"chk&gt;grow;grow&gt;comp;comp&gt;defrag;defrag&gt;alarm\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">etcd operations: health, compaction and defragmentation\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"chk\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Health and status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"grow\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database growth\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Compaction\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"defrag\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Defragmentation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alarm\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">NOSPACE alarm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Health and status to Database growth; 2, Database growth to Compaction; 3, Compaction to Defragmentation; 4, Defragmentation to NOSPACE alarm.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"chk\">\u003cdt>Health and status \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Weekly checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"grow\">\u003cdt>Database growth \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Revisions pile up.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hot objects.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comp\">\u003cdt>Compaction \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Frees old revisions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not run.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"defrag\">\u003cdt>Defragmentation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Returns disk space.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Run on all at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alarm\">\u003cdt>NOSPACE alarm \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rejects writes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not disarmed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-database-space-exceeded\">Incident: Database space exceeded\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Cluster changes started failing with an etcd space error after a noisy controller created millions of events.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create namespace test\netcdctl alarm list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl create namespace \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server: etcdserver: mvcc: database space exceeded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>etcdctl alarm list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">memberID:8e9e05c52164694d alarm:NOSPACE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The data file had reached its quota and etcd had gone into protection mode. The team compacted to the current revision, defragmented members one at a time, disarmed the alarm and then fixed the controller that created the events. Raising the quota alone would only have delayed the next alarm. Always find and fix the writer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Defragmenting every member at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Raising the quota without finding the cause\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to disarm the alarm after cleaning up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not checking health after maintenance\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring growth until writes fail\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"routine-checks","text":"Routine checks"},{"id":"why-the-database-grows","text":"Why the database grows"},{"id":"compaction-is-not-defragmentation","text":"Compaction is not defragmentation"},{"id":"the-space-quota-alarm","text":"The space quota alarm"},{"id":"what-to-alert-on","text":"What to alert on"},{"id":"incident-database-space-exceeded","text":"Incident: Database space exceeded"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"etcd maintenance","u":"https://etcd.io/docs/latest/op-guide/maintenance/"},{"t":"Operating etcd clusters for Kubernetes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/"}],"text":"Most etcd incidents are boring and preventable: a database that grew past its quota, a slow disk, or a member that fell behind. This lesson covers the routine checks and the exact steps to recover from the most common failure, the space quota alarm. What you will learn Reading endpoint status, health and alarms Why the database grows and what compaction does Defragmentation and when it is safe The space quota alarm and how to clear it Metrics worth alerting on Routine checks Run three commands regularly, with the same endpoint and certificate flags you use for backups. endpoint health tests that each member can commit. endpoint status shows database size, leader and raft index for each member. alarm list shows any active alarm. export ETCDCTL_API=3 E= \"--endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key\" etcdctl $E endpoint health etcdctl $E endpoint status -w table etcdctl $E alarm list Why the database grows Because etcd keeps old revisions, the data file grows with every change, and objects that change often, such as leases and events, create many revisions. Compaction discards revisions older than a chosen one. The API server compacts automatically on a schedule, by default every five minutes, so growth normally stays bounded. Compaction is not defragmentation Compaction marks old revisions as free, but the file on disk does not shrink. Free space inside the file is reused for new data. Defragmentation rewrites the file to give space back to the filesystem. It blocks the member while it runs, so defragment one member at a time, and not the leader first. etcdctl $E defrag --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 The space quota alarm etcd has a backend quota, 2 GiB by default and commonly raised to 8 GiB. If the data file reaches it, etcd raises a NOSPACE alarm and rejects writes, with the error \"mvcc: database space exceeded\". Recovery is: find the current revision, compact to it, defragment each member, then disarm the alarm. REV=$(etcdctl $E endpoint status -w json | grep -o &#x27;\"revision\":[0-9]*&#x27; | head -1 | cut -d: -f2) etcdctl $E compact $REV etcdctl $E defrag etcdctl $E alarm disarm What to alert on Alert on database size close to the quota, on a rising rate of leader changes, on high wal_fsync and backend_commit latency, and on failed proposals. Also watch the number of objects, since a runaway controller that creates many events or secrets is the usual cause of growth. etcd operations: health, compaction and defragmentation Flow in order: 1, Health and status to Database growth; 2, Database growth to Compaction; 3, Compaction to Defragmentation; 4, Defragmentation to NOSPACE alarm. Observability Data store Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Health and status Observability What it does: Weekly checks. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Database growth Data store What it does: Revisions pile up. If it fails or is weak: Hot objects. Compaction Step What it does: Frees old revisions. If it fails or is weak: Not run. Defragmentation Step What it does: Returns disk space. If it fails or is weak: Run on all at once. NOSPACE alarm Observability What it does: Rejects writes. If it fails or is weak: Not disarmed. Incident: Database space exceeded Incident Cluster changes started failing with an etcd space error after a noisy controller created millions of events. $ kubectl create namespace test Error from server: etcdserver: mvcc: database space exceeded $ etcdctl alarm list memberID:8e9e05c52164694d alarm:NOSPACE (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The data file had reached its quota and etcd had gone into protection mode. The team compacted to the current revision, defragmented members one at a time, disarmed the alarm and then fixed the controller that created the events. Raising the quota alone would only have de"},"k8s-control-plane/the-scheduler-filtering-scoring-and-binding":{"html":"\u003cp>The scheduler has one job: assign each unscheduled pod to a node. It does this in a predictable cycle, and every decision leaves evidence in pod events. Once you know the stages, a Pending pod stops being mysterious.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The scheduling queue and the cycle of filter, score and bind\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What the main filter and score plugins check\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preemption and priority\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scheduler profiles and extension points\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading FailedScheduling events\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cycle\">The cycle\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pods without a node name enter a queue ordered by priority. For each pod the scheduler runs a scheduling cycle. First, filtering removes nodes that cannot run the pod. Second, scoring ranks the remaining nodes. The best node is chosen, with ties broken at random. Finally, a binding cycle writes the node name into the pod through the API server, and the kubelet on that node takes over.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"filtering\">Filtering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Filter plugins check hard constraints. NodeResourcesFit checks that requests fit the free capacity, NodeAffinity and NodeSelector check labels, TaintToleration checks taints, VolumeBinding checks that claims can be satisfied and PodTopologySpread checks skew limits. A node that fails any filter is out. If every node is filtered out the pod stays Pending and the scheduler records an event that counts the reasons.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scoring\">Scoring\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Score plugins rank nodes by preference, for example spreading pods across nodes, favouring nodes that already have the image, or balancing resource use. Scores are weighted and summed. Scoring cannot rescue a pod that failed filtering, it only chooses between valid nodes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preemption\">Preemption\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If no node fits and the pod has a higher priority than some running pods, the scheduler can choose a node where evicting lower priority pods would make room. It marks the victims for deletion and records a nominated node for the new pod. Priority classes control this, and critical system pods use very high priorities.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"profiles-and-extension\">Profiles and extension\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The scheduling framework exposes extension points such as filter, score and bind. A scheduler configuration file can enable or disable plugins and set weights, and several profiles can run in one scheduler, selected by \u003ccode>schedulerName\u003c/code> in the pod. Running a second scheduler is possible but rarely needed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl describe pod web-1 | sed -n '/Events/,$p'\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler --tail=20\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl describe pod web-1 | sed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/Events/,$p&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>=20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"q&gt;f;f&gt;s;s&gt;b;b&gt;k\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The scheduler: filtering, scoring and binding\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"f\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Filter\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"s\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Score\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"b\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bind\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"k\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Queue to Filter; 2, Filter to Score; 3, Score to Bind; 4, Bind to kubelet.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ordered by priority.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Starved low priority.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"f\">\u003cdt>Filter \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Removes unfit nodes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Taints and selectors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"s\">\u003cdt>Score \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ranks the rest.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skewed weights.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"b\">\u003cdt>Bind \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Writes the node name.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> API conflict.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"k\">\u003cdt>kubelet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Starts the pod.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Image pull errors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-scheduler-that-stopped-scheduling\">Incident: A scheduler that stopped scheduling\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A typo in a scheduler configuration file left every new pod Pending while existing pods ran normally.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler\nkubectl get pods\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                              READY   STATUS             RESTARTS   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kube-scheduler-cp1                0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   14         32m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME    READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web-1   0/1     Pending   0          9m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The scheduler is a static pod, and an edit to its configuration file had an unknown field, so the process exited at start. Pods without a node stayed Pending because nothing was binding them, with no scheduling events at all, which is the clue. Restoring the backed-up manifest brought it back. An empty events list on a Pending pod means the scheduler did not see it, not that it rejected it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Changing resource limits when the event names a taint\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming scoring can override a failed filter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing a scheduler manifest without keeping a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading Pending as a node problem when the scheduler is down\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that priority only matters when capacity is short\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-cycle","text":"The cycle"},{"id":"filtering","text":"Filtering"},{"id":"scoring","text":"Scoring"},{"id":"preemption","text":"Preemption"},{"id":"profiles-and-extension","text":"Profiles and extension"},{"id":"incident-a-scheduler-that-stopped-scheduling","text":"Incident: A scheduler that stopped scheduling"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes scheduler","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/kube-scheduler/"},{"t":"Scheduling framework","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/scheduling-framework/"}],"text":"The scheduler has one job: assign each unscheduled pod to a node. It does this in a predictable cycle, and every decision leaves evidence in pod events. Once you know the stages, a Pending pod stops being mysterious. What you will learn The scheduling queue and the cycle of filter, score and bind What the main filter and score plugins check Preemption and priority Scheduler profiles and extension points Reading FailedScheduling events The cycle Pods without a node name enter a queue ordered by priority. For each pod the scheduler runs a scheduling cycle. First, filtering removes nodes that cannot run the pod. Second, scoring ranks the remaining nodes. The best node is chosen, with ties broken at random. Finally, a binding cycle writes the node name into the pod through the API server, and the kubelet on that node takes over. Filtering Filter plugins check hard constraints. NodeResourcesFit checks that requests fit the free capacity, NodeAffinity and NodeSelector check labels, TaintToleration checks taints, VolumeBinding checks that claims can be satisfied and PodTopologySpread checks skew limits. A node that fails any filter is out. If every node is filtered out the pod stays Pending and the scheduler records an event that counts the reasons. Scoring Score plugins rank nodes by preference, for example spreading pods across nodes, favouring nodes that already have the image, or balancing resource use. Scores are weighted and summed. Scoring cannot rescue a pod that failed filtering, it only chooses between valid nodes. Preemption If no node fits and the pod has a higher priority than some running pods, the scheduler can choose a node where evicting lower priority pods would make room. It marks the victims for deletion and records a nominated node for the new pod. Priority classes control this, and critical system pods use very high priorities. Profiles and extension The scheduling framework exposes extension points such as filter, score and bind. A scheduler configuration file can enable or disable plugins and set weights, and several profiles can run in one scheduler, selected by schedulerName in the pod. Running a second scheduler is possible but rarely needed. kubectl describe pod web-1 | sed -n &#x27;/Events/,$p&#x27; kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler kubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler -- tail =20 The scheduler: filtering, scoring and binding Flow in order: 1, Queue to Filter; 2, Filter to Score; 3, Score to Bind; 4, Bind to kubelet. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Queue Data store What it does: Ordered by priority. If it fails or is weak: Starved low priority. Filter Step What it does: Removes unfit nodes. If it fails or is weak: Taints and selectors. Score Step What it does: Ranks the rest. If it fails or is weak: Skewed weights. Bind Service What it does: Writes the node name. If it fails or is weak: API conflict. kubelet Service What it does: Starts the pod. If it fails or is weak: Image pull errors. Incident: A scheduler that stopped scheduling Incident A typo in a scheduler configuration file left every new pod Pending while existing pods ran normally. $ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE kube-scheduler-cp1 0/1 CrashLoopBackOff 14 32m $ kubectl get pods NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS AGE web-1 0/1 Pending 0 9m (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The scheduler is a static pod, and an edit to its configuration file had an unknown field, so the process exited at start. Pods without a node stayed Pending because nothing was binding them, with no scheduling events at all, which is the clue. Restoring the backed-up manifest brought it back. An empty events list on a Pending pod means the scheduler did not see it, not that it rejected it. Common mistakes Changing resource limits when the event names a ta"},"k8s-control-plane/controllers-the-reconcile-loop-and-leader-election":{"html":"\u003cp>Kubernetes is a set of control loops. You declare what you want, and controllers keep comparing it with what exists and acting on the difference. When something does not scale, restart or clean up, the explanation is almost always in a controller, so it is worth knowing how they work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The observe, compare, act loop and level-triggered design\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Informers, caches and work queues\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The built-in controllers and the controller manager\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Owner references and garbage collection\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leader election with Leases\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"level-triggered-control\">Level-triggered control\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A controller does not react to each event as a command. It reads the current desired state and the current actual state and acts to close the gap. If it misses an event or restarts, it simply looks again, so the system recovers by design. This is why Kubernetes objects have a spec for what you want and a status for what exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"informers-and-work-queues\">Informers and work queues\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A controller uses an informer to list and watch a resource type and keep a local cache. Changes put the object key into a rate limited work queue. A worker takes a key, reads the object from the cache, reconciles, and either finishes or requeues with backoff. Using the cache keeps load off the API server.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-controller-manager\">The controller manager\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kube-controller-manager runs many controllers in one process: the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Job, Node, Endpoints and ServiceAccount controllers, among others. A Deployment controller creates ReplicaSets, a ReplicaSet controller creates pods, and so on, so a single scale request fans out through several loops. The cloud-controller-manager does the same for cloud specific resources such as load balancers and node lifecycle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"owner-references-and-garbage-collection\">Owner references and garbage collection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Objects created by a controller carry an ownerReference to their parent. When the parent is deleted, the garbage collector deletes dependents, in background by default or foreground if requested. That is why deleting a Deployment removes its pods, and why an orphaned object may point to an owner that no longer exists.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"leader-election\">Leader election\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Control plane components usually run more than one copy for availability, but only one controller manager or scheduler may act at a time. Each copy tries to hold a Lease object in kube-system, renewing it before it expires. The holder is the leader. If it stops renewing, another copy takes over after the lease duration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-controller-manager -o jsonpath='{.spec.holderIdentity}{&quot;\\n&quot;}'\nkubectl get lease -n kube-system\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-controller-manager -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.holderIdentity}{&quot;\\n&quot;}&#x27;\u003c/span>\nkubectl get lease -n kube-system\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"spec&gt;inf;inf&gt;q;q&gt;rec;rec&gt;act\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Controllers: the reconcile loop and leader election\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"spec\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Desired state\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"inf\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Informer cache\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Work queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rec\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reconcile\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Act via API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Desired state to Informer cache; 2, Informer cache to Work queue; 3, Work queue to Reconcile; 4, Reconcile to Act via API.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"spec\">\u003cdt>Desired state \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Written by users.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong spec.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"inf\">\u003cdt>Informer cache \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Watches and caches.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale cache.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Work queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Rate limited keys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hot loop.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rec\">\u003cdt>Reconcile \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Closes the gap.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Error loops.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Act via API \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates or deletes objects.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Permission denied.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-leader-flapping-on-a-loaded-control-plane\">Incident: Leader flapping on a loaded control plane\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The controller manager kept changing leaders, and deployments scaled slowly.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-controller-manager -o jsonpath='{.spec.leaseTransitions}'\nkubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager --tail=3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-controller-manager -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.leaseTransitions}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">187\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>=3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">E1114 08:12:01 leaderelection.go:332] error retrieving resource lock kube-system/kube-controller-manager: context deadline exceeded\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The active controller could not renew its Lease because API requests to the server were slow, so it lost leadership, and another copy had to start its caches before acting. Each transition delayed reconciliation. The cause was an overloaded API server with a slow etcd disk. Fixing the storage latency stopped the flapping. Leader election problems usually point to slow API or etcd rather than a bug in the controller.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Expecting controllers to act on a single event rather than on state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing a ReplicaSet owned by a Deployment and expecting it to stick\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring leaseTransitions as a health signal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting an owner without checking the dependents\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running controllers that write every second without backoff\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"level-triggered-control","text":"Level-triggered control"},{"id":"informers-and-work-queues","text":"Informers and work queues"},{"id":"the-controller-manager","text":"The controller manager"},{"id":"owner-references-and-garbage-collection","text":"Owner references and garbage collection"},{"id":"leader-election","text":"Leader election"},{"id":"incident-leader-flapping-on-a-loaded-control-plane","text":"Incident: Leader flapping on a loaded control plane"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Controllers","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/controller/"},{"t":"Leases","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/leases/"}],"text":"Kubernetes is a set of control loops. You declare what you want, and controllers keep comparing it with what exists and acting on the difference. When something does not scale, restart or clean up, the explanation is almost always in a controller, so it is worth knowing how they work. What you will learn The observe, compare, act loop and level-triggered design Informers, caches and work queues The built-in controllers and the controller manager Owner references and garbage collection Leader election with Leases Level-triggered control A controller does not react to each event as a command. It reads the current desired state and the current actual state and acts to close the gap. If it misses an event or restarts, it simply looks again, so the system recovers by design. This is why Kubernetes objects have a spec for what you want and a status for what exists. Informers and work queues A controller uses an informer to list and watch a resource type and keep a local cache. Changes put the object key into a rate limited work queue. A worker takes a key, reads the object from the cache, reconciles, and either finishes or requeues with backoff. Using the cache keeps load off the API server. The controller manager The kube-controller-manager runs many controllers in one process: the Deployment, ReplicaSet, Job, Node, Endpoints and ServiceAccount controllers, among others. A Deployment controller creates ReplicaSets, a ReplicaSet controller creates pods, and so on, so a single scale request fans out through several loops. The cloud-controller-manager does the same for cloud specific resources such as load balancers and node lifecycle. Owner references and garbage collection Objects created by a controller carry an ownerReference to their parent. When the parent is deleted, the garbage collector deletes dependents, in background by default or foreground if requested. That is why deleting a Deployment removes its pods, and why an orphaned object may point to an owner that no longer exists. Leader election Control plane components usually run more than one copy for availability, but only one controller manager or scheduler may act at a time. Each copy tries to hold a Lease object in kube-system, renewing it before it expires. The holder is the leader. If it stops renewing, another copy takes over after the lease duration. kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-controller-manager -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.holderIdentity}{\"\\n\"}&#x27; kubectl get lease -n kube-system Controllers: the reconcile loop and leader election Flow in order: 1, Desired state to Informer cache; 2, Informer cache to Work queue; 3, Work queue to Reconcile; 4, Reconcile to Act via API. Data store Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Desired state Data store What it does: Written by users. If it fails or is weak: Wrong spec. Informer cache Data store What it does: Watches and caches. If it fails or is weak: Stale cache. Work queue Step What it does: Rate limited keys. If it fails or is weak: Hot loop. Reconcile Step What it does: Closes the gap. If it fails or is weak: Error loops. Act via API Service What it does: Creates or deletes objects. If it fails or is weak: Permission denied. Incident: Leader flapping on a loaded control plane Incident The controller manager kept changing leaders, and deployments scaled slowly. $ kubectl get lease -n kube-system kube-controller-manager -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.leaseTransitions}&#x27; 187 $ kubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager -- tail =3 E1114 08:12:01 leaderelection.go:332] error retrieving resource lock kube-system/kube-controller-manager: context deadline exceeded (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The active controller could not renew its Lease because API requests to the server were slow, so it lost leadership, and another copy had to start its caches before acting. Each transition delay"},"k8s-control-plane/the-kubelet-pod-lifecycle-and-the-cri":{"html":"\u003cp>The control plane decides, and the kubelet does. It is the agent on every node that turns pod specifications into running containers and reports what is actually happening. Most node level problems, from pods stuck in ContainerCreating to NotReady nodes, are kubelet problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The sync loop and how the kubelet gets its pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The CRI, CNI and CSI calls during pod start\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pod phases, container states and probes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Node status, heartbeats and eviction\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Static pods and the kubelet configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-pods-come-from\">Where pods come from\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet watches the API server for pods bound to its node. It also reads static pod manifests from a local directory and optionally an HTTP source. For each pod it runs a sync loop that compares the desired containers with what the runtime reports and takes action to match.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"starting-a-pod\">Starting a pod\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet asks the container runtime, through the Container Runtime Interface, to create a pod sandbox. A CNI plugin then sets up the pod network and assigns an IP. Volumes are mounted, with CSI drivers for dynamic storage. Images are pulled, then containers are created and started in order, init containers first. Each step can fail visibly: ContainerCreating with a network or mount event, ImagePullBackOff, or CrashLoopBackOff.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"phases-states-and-probes\">Phases, states and probes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pod phase is Pending, Running, Succeeded, Failed or Unknown. Each container is Waiting, Running or Terminated, with a reason. Startup, liveness and readiness probes let the kubelet decide whether to restart a container or whether to include it in service endpoints. A failing readiness probe removes the pod from endpoints without restarting it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"node-status-and-heartbeats\">Node status and heartbeats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet updates the node object conditions such as Ready, MemoryPressure and DiskPressure, and renews a Lease in the kube-node-lease namespace as a cheap heartbeat. If the control plane stops seeing heartbeats for the grace period, the node controller marks the node NotReady and later taints it so pods are evicted. Under pressure the kubelet itself evicts pods, lowest priority and highest overuse first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configuration-and-diagnosis\">Configuration and diagnosis\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The kubelet runs as a systemd service on the host, not as a pod. Its settings live in a config file, typically \u003ccode>/var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl status kubelet\njournalctl -u kubelet --since &quot;10 min ago&quot; | tail -20\ncrictl ps -a | head\ngrep staticPodPath /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">systemctl status kubelet\njournalctl -u kubelet --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10 min ago&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -20\ncrictl ps -a | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\ngrep staticPodPath /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"api&gt;kl;kl&gt;cri;kl&gt;cni;kl&gt;csi\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The kubelet: pod lifecycle and the container runtime interface\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">API server\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cri\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:16.7%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Container runtime\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cni\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:50.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CNI plugin\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"csi\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:83.3%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">CSI driver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, API server to kubelet; 2, kubelet to Container runtime; 3, kubelet to CNI plugin; 4, kubelet to CSI driver.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>API server \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Source of pods.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unreachable.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kl\">\u003cdt>kubelet \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sync loop.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stopped service.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cri\">\u003cdt>Container runtime \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates containers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Runtime down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cni\">\u003cdt>CNI plugin \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pod network.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing config.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"csi\">\u003cdt>CSI driver \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Volumes.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Mount error.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-node-that-went-notready-after-a-reboot\">Incident: The node that went NotReady after a reboot\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A worker came back from a reboot as NotReady and its pods were rescheduled.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get nodes\nsystemctl is-active containerd kubelet\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get nodes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME       STATUS     ROLES    AGE   VERSION\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">worker-2   NotReady   &lt;none&gt;   90d   v1.31.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl is-active containerd kubelet\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">active\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">inactive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The container runtime started, but the kubelet service was not enabled to start at boot, so no heartbeats reached the control plane. After the grace period the node controller marked the node NotReady and evicted its pods. Starting and enabling the kubelet fixed it. Check the lowest layer first: is the service running, and is it enabled.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looking in kubectl before checking the kubelet service on a NotReady node\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that readiness failures remove endpoints but do not restart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing the pod phase with the container state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing static pod manifests without a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not enabling the kubelet and runtime services at boot\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-pods-come-from","text":"Where pods come from"},{"id":"starting-a-pod","text":"Starting a pod"},{"id":"phases-states-and-probes","text":"Phases, states and probes"},{"id":"node-status-and-heartbeats","text":"Node status and heartbeats"},{"id":"configuration-and-diagnosis","text":"Configuration and diagnosis"},{"id":"incident-the-node-that-went-notready-after-a-reboot","text":"Incident: The node that went NotReady after a reboot"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubelet","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/command-line-tools-reference/kubelet/"},{"t":"Container runtimes","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/container-runtimes/"}],"text":"The control plane decides, and the kubelet does. It is the agent on every node that turns pod specifications into running containers and reports what is actually happening. Most node level problems, from pods stuck in ContainerCreating to NotReady nodes, are kubelet problems. What you will learn The sync loop and how the kubelet gets its pods The CRI, CNI and CSI calls during pod start Pod phases, container states and probes Node status, heartbeats and eviction Static pods and the kubelet configuration Where pods come from The kubelet watches the API server for pods bound to its node. It also reads static pod manifests from a local directory and optionally an HTTP source. For each pod it runs a sync loop that compares the desired containers with what the runtime reports and takes action to match. Starting a pod The kubelet asks the container runtime, through the Container Runtime Interface, to create a pod sandbox. A CNI plugin then sets up the pod network and assigns an IP. Volumes are mounted, with CSI drivers for dynamic storage. Images are pulled, then containers are created and started in order, init containers first. Each step can fail visibly: ContainerCreating with a network or mount event, ImagePullBackOff, or CrashLoopBackOff. Phases, states and probes A pod phase is Pending, Running, Succeeded, Failed or Unknown. Each container is Waiting, Running or Terminated, with a reason. Startup, liveness and readiness probes let the kubelet decide whether to restart a container or whether to include it in service endpoints. A failing readiness probe removes the pod from endpoints without restarting it. Node status and heartbeats The kubelet updates the node object conditions such as Ready, MemoryPressure and DiskPressure, and renews a Lease in the kube-node-lease namespace as a cheap heartbeat. If the control plane stops seeing heartbeats for the grace period, the node controller marks the node NotReady and later taints it so pods are evicted. Under pressure the kubelet itself evicts pods, lowest priority and highest overuse first. Configuration and diagnosis The kubelet runs as a systemd service on the host, not as a pod. Its settings live in a config file, typically /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml . systemctl status kubelet journalctl -u kubelet --since \"10 min ago\" | tail -20 crictl ps -a | head grep staticPodPath /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml The kubelet: pod lifecycle and the container runtime interface Flow in order: 1, API server to kubelet; 2, kubelet to Container runtime; 3, kubelet to CNI plugin; 4, kubelet to CSI driver. Service Step Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. API server Service What it does: Source of pods. If it fails or is weak: Unreachable. kubelet Service What it does: Sync loop. If it fails or is weak: Stopped service. Container runtime Step What it does: Creates containers. If it fails or is weak: Runtime down. CNI plugin Step What it does: Pod network. If it fails or is weak: Missing config. CSI driver Data store What it does: Volumes. If it fails or is weak: Mount error. Incident: The node that went NotReady after a reboot Incident A worker came back from a reboot as NotReady and its pods were rescheduled. $ kubectl get nodes NAME STATUS ROLES AGE VERSION worker-2 NotReady \u003cnone> 90d v1.31.2 $ systemctl is-active containerd kubelet active inactive (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The container runtime started, but the kubelet service was not enabled to start at boot, so no heartbeats reached the control plane. After the grace period the node controller marked the node NotReady and evicted its pods. Starting and enabling the kubelet fixed it. Check the lowest layer first: is the service running, and is it enabled. Common mistakes Looking in kubectl before checking the kubelet service on a NotReady node Forgetting that readiness failures remove endpoints but do not restart Confusing the pod ph"},"k8s-control-plane/control-plane-failure-modes-and-diagnosis":{"html":"\u003cp>A control plane failure feels dramatic, but each component has a narrow job, so each failure has a narrow signature. Knowing what keeps working is the fastest route to the cause, and it also tells you how urgent the problem is.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What breaks and what survives for each component\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Symptoms that point to the API server, etcd, scheduler, controller manager or kubelet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A fixed diagnosis order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The tools you can use when kubectl is unavailable\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-failure-matrix\">The failure matrix\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Running pods keep running in nearly every control plane failure, because the kubelet and the container runtime work from what they already have. What stops is change. If etcd loses quorum, nothing can be written. If the API server is down, nobody can read or write and controllers cannot act, but pods and Services keep working. If the scheduler is down, existing pods run and new pods stay Pending with no events. If the controller manager is down, deployments do not scale, replacements are not created and nodes are never marked NotReady. If a kubelet is down, only that node is affected.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-the-symptoms\">Reading the symptoms\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>kubectl timeouts or connection refused point to the API server or the network path to it. A &quot;server is currently unable to handle the request&quot; or etcd errors point to etcd. Pending pods without events point to the scheduler. A Deployment that does not create ReplicaSets points to the controller manager. A single NotReady node points to its kubelet, runtime or network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"diagnosis-order\">Diagnosis order\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Work from the bottom up. First the node: is the kubelet running, and is the container runtime running. Next the static pods: do the manifests exist, and does \u003ccode>crictl ps\u003c/code> show the containers. Then each component log, starting with etcd, then the API server, then the scheduler and controller manager. Make one change at a time, and re-check the symptom after each.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-kubectl-does-not-work\">When kubectl does not work\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use the tools on the control plane node itself. \u003ccode>crictl ps -a\u003c/code> lists containers, \u003ccode>crictl logs\u003c/code> reads their logs, \u003ccode>journalctl -u kubelet\u003c/code> shows why the kubelet could not start a static pod, and \u003ccode>ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests\u003c/code> shows the manifests. Health endpoints can be queried directly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"crictl ps -a | grep -E &quot;etcd|apiserver|scheduler|controller&quot;\ncurl -k https://127.0.0.1:6443/livez?verbose | tail -5\nls -l /etc/kubernetes/manifests\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">crictl ps -a | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;etcd|apiserver|scheduler|controller&quot;\u003c/span>\ncurl -k https://127.0.0.1:6443/livez?verbose | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -5\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /etc/kubernetes/manifests\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-quick-reference-in-code\">A quick reference in code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"failure = {\n    &quot;etcd quorum lost&quot;: &quot;no writes, pods keep running&quot;,\n    &quot;api server down&quot;: &quot;no kubectl, pods and services keep running&quot;,\n    &quot;scheduler down&quot;: &quot;new pods Pending with no events&quot;,\n    &quot;controller manager down&quot;: &quot;no scaling, no replacement, no node eviction&quot;,\n    &quot;one kubelet down&quot;: &quot;that node NotReady, others fine&quot;,\n}\nfor k, v in failure.items():\n    print(k, &quot;-&gt;&quot;, v)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">failure = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;etcd quorum lost&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;no writes, pods keep running&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;api server down&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;no kubectl, pods and services keep running&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;scheduler down&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new pods Pending with no events&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;controller manager down&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;no scaling, no replacement, no node eviction&quot;\u003c/span>,\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;one kubelet down&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;that node NotReady, others fine&quot;\u003c/span>,\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k, v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> failure.items():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(k, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;-&gt;&quot;\u003c/span>, v)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"sym&gt;node;node&gt;static;static&gt;logs;logs&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Control plane failure modes and diagnosis\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sym\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Symptom\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node layer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"static\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Static pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"logs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Component logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">One change\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Symptom to Node layer; 2, Node layer to Static pods; 3, Static pods to Component logs; 4, Component logs to One change.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sym\">\u003cdt>Symptom \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> What the user sees.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Vague report.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Node layer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> kubelet and runtime.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Service disabled.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"static\">\u003cdt>Static pods \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Manifests and containers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Bad edit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"logs\">\u003cdt>Component logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> etcd then API then rest.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong order.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>One change \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Then re-check.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Many changes at once.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-deployments-that-would-not-scale\">Incident: Deployments that would not scale\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team saw deployments ignore scale requests while all existing pods stayed healthy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl scale deploy web --replicas=5\nkubectl get deploy web\nkubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl scale deploy web --replicas=5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deployment.apps/web scaled\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">web    2/2     2            2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME                          READY   STATUS             RESTARTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kube-controller-manager-cp1   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   9\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The API accepted the new replica count, but the controller manager that would create the ReplicaSet change was crash looping because of an expired client certificate path in its manifest. Existing pods ran normally. The log showed the exact file it could not read, and correcting the manifest restored scaling. A request that the API accepts but nothing acts on points to a controller, not the API.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming an outage means pods have stopped\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing several things before re-checking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Looking only at kubectl when it may be the failing component\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the node layer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not keeping copies of manifests before edits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-failure-matrix","text":"The failure matrix"},{"id":"reading-the-symptoms","text":"Reading the symptoms"},{"id":"diagnosis-order","text":"Diagnosis order"},{"id":"when-kubectl-does-not-work","text":"When kubectl does not work"},{"id":"a-quick-reference-in-code","text":"A quick reference in code"},{"id":"incident-deployments-that-would-not-scale","text":"Incident: Deployments that would not scale"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Troubleshooting clusters","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-cluster/"},{"t":"Kubernetes components","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/components/"}],"text":"A control plane failure feels dramatic, but each component has a narrow job, so each failure has a narrow signature. Knowing what keeps working is the fastest route to the cause, and it also tells you how urgent the problem is. What you will learn What breaks and what survives for each component Symptoms that point to the API server, etcd, scheduler, controller manager or kubelet A fixed diagnosis order The tools you can use when kubectl is unavailable The failure matrix Running pods keep running in nearly every control plane failure, because the kubelet and the container runtime work from what they already have. What stops is change. If etcd loses quorum, nothing can be written. If the API server is down, nobody can read or write and controllers cannot act, but pods and Services keep working. If the scheduler is down, existing pods run and new pods stay Pending with no events. If the controller manager is down, deployments do not scale, replacements are not created and nodes are never marked NotReady. If a kubelet is down, only that node is affected. Reading the symptoms kubectl timeouts or connection refused point to the API server or the network path to it. A \"server is currently unable to handle the request\" or etcd errors point to etcd. Pending pods without events point to the scheduler. A Deployment that does not create ReplicaSets points to the controller manager. A single NotReady node points to its kubelet, runtime or network. Diagnosis order Work from the bottom up. First the node: is the kubelet running, and is the container runtime running. Next the static pods: do the manifests exist, and does crictl ps show the containers. Then each component log, starting with etcd, then the API server, then the scheduler and controller manager. Make one change at a time, and re-check the symptom after each. When kubectl does not work Use the tools on the control plane node itself. crictl ps -a lists containers, crictl logs reads their logs, journalctl -u kubelet shows why the kubelet could not start a static pod, and ls /etc/kubernetes/manifests shows the manifests. Health endpoints can be queried directly. crictl ps -a | grep -E \"etcd|apiserver|scheduler|controller\" curl -k https://127.0.0.1:6443/livez?verbose | tail -5 ls -l /etc/kubernetes/manifests A quick reference in code failure = { \"etcd quorum lost\" : \"no writes, pods keep running\" , \"api server down\" : \"no kubectl, pods and services keep running\" , \"scheduler down\" : \"new pods Pending with no events\" , \"controller manager down\" : \"no scaling, no replacement, no node eviction\" , \"one kubelet down\" : \"that node NotReady, others fine\" , } for k, v in failure.items(): print (k, \"->\" , v) Control plane failure modes and diagnosis Flow in order: 1, Symptom to Node layer; 2, Node layer to Static pods; 3, Static pods to Component logs; 4, Component logs to One change. Observability Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Symptom Observability What it does: What the user sees. If it fails or is weak: Vague report. Node layer Step What it does: kubelet and runtime. If it fails or is weak: Service disabled. Static pods Step What it does: Manifests and containers. If it fails or is weak: Bad edit. Component logs Observability What it does: etcd then API then rest. If it fails or is weak: Wrong order. One change Service What it does: Then re-check. If it fails or is weak: Many changes at once. Incident: Deployments that would not scale Incident A team saw deployments ignore scale requests while all existing pods stayed healthy. $ kubectl scale deploy web --replicas=5 deployment.apps/web scaled $ kubectl get deploy web NAME READY UP-TO-DATE AVAILABLE web 2/2 2 2 $ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-controller-manager NAME READY STATUS RESTARTS kube-controller-manager-cp1 0/1 CrashLoopBackOff 9 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The API accepted the new replica "}});